---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { dispensaries } from "../data/dispensaries";
import { SITE_URL } from "../config/site";

const total = dispensaries.length;
const towns = new Set(dispensaries.map((d) => d.town)).size;
const counties = new Set(dispensaries.map((d) => d.county)).size;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Is cannabis legal in Vermont?",
    a: "Yes. Adults 21 and over can legally possess and buy cannabis from licensed retailers in Vermont. Possession was legalised in 2018 and licensed retail sales began on 1 October 2022. Cannabis remains illegal under federal law.",
  },
  {
    q: "How much cannabis can I buy or carry in Vermont?",
    a: "Adults 21 and over may possess up to one ounce of cannabis flower or five grams of hashish. Dispensaries will not sell you more than the possession limit in a single transaction.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need to be a Vermont resident to buy cannabis?",
    a: "No. Any adult 21 or over with valid government-issued photo ID can buy from a Vermont dispensary, regardless of what state they live in. You cannot legally take it across a state line.",
  },
  {
    q: "How much is cannabis taxed in Vermont?",
    a: "Vermont applies a 14% cannabis excise tax on top of the 6% state sales tax, so expect roughly 20% added at the register. Most menu prices are shown before tax.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I grow cannabis at home in Vermont?",
    a: "Yes. Vermont allows adults 21 and over to grow up to two mature and four immature plants per dwelling, not per person. Landlords may prohibit it in rental agreements.",
  },
  {
    q: "Why does my town not have a dispensary?",
    a: "Vermont requires towns to affirmatively vote to permit cannabis retail. A town that has never held that vote cannot host a dispensary, regardless of state licensing. That single rule explains most of the gaps on the map.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I use cannabis in public in Vermont?",
    a: "No. Public consumption is illegal, including in streets, parks and vehicles. Consumption is limited to private property, subject to the property owner's rules.",
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
    })),
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: `${SITE_URL}/` },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Vermont Cannabis Guide", item: `${SITE_URL}/vermont-cannabis-guide/` },
    ],
  },
];

const toc = [
  ["the-law", "What the law actually says"],
  ["buying", "Buying: what to bring and expect"],
  ["potency", "Potency caps and reading a label"],
  ["taxes", "Taxes and paying"],
  ["where", "Where the dispensaries are"],
  ["medical", "The medical program"],
  ["home-grow", "Growing at home"],
  ["faq", "Common questions"],
];
---

<Page
  title="Vermont Cannabis Guide: Laws, Buying & Dispensaries | 2026"
  description="A plain-English guide to legal cannabis in Vermont: possession limits, potency caps, taxes, where dispensaries are allowed, home growing, and what to expect on your first visit."
  label="Guide"
  heading="The Vermont cannabis guide"
  intro="Everything a Vermont buyer actually needs to know, without the legislative jargon. Last reviewed August 2026."
  schema={schema}
>
  <nav class="card p-5 mb-10">
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-brand-muted mb-3">On this page</p>
    <ol class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6 gap-y-1.5 text-sm">
      {toc.map(([id, label], i) => (
        <li>
          <a href={`#${id}`} class="text-brand-green font-semibold hover:underline">
            {i + 1}. {label}
          </a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="the-law">What the law actually says</h2>
    <p>
      Vermont legalised possession of small amounts of cannabis in 2018 under Act 86, but that
      law created no legal way to buy any. For four years the state had legal possession and no
      legal supply, which is a strange thing to legislate for and was widely recognised as such.
    </p>
    <p>
      Act 164, passed in 2020, fixed it by creating a regulated commercial market overseen by
      the Cannabis Control Board. Licensed retail sales began on 1 October 2022.
    </p>
    <p>The core rules for adults 21 and over:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Possess up to <strong>one ounce</strong> of flower, or five grams of hashish</li>
      <li>Buy from any <strong>licensed retailer</strong>, with no residency requirement</li>
      <li>Grow up to <strong>two mature and four immature plants</strong> per dwelling</li>
      <li><strong>No public consumption</strong>, anywhere, including in a parked car</li>
      <li><strong>No crossing state lines</strong>, which is a federal offence</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Driving under the influence is treated like alcohol impairment and is prosecuted. There is
      no legal threshold that makes it acceptable.
    </p>

    <h2 id="buying">Buying: what to bring and expect</h2>
    <p>
      Bring government-issued photo ID showing you are 21 or over. Every licensed shop checks it
      every visit. There is no discretion on this and no amount of obviously being over 40 gets
      you past it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Beyond that, a Vermont dispensary is an unremarkable retail experience. You will usually
      be greeted, sometimes asked to wait if the shop limits how many customers are on the floor,
      and then helped by a budtender. Ask them questions. They deal with beginners constantly and
      the good ones will steer you toward something milder than you think you want.
    </p>
    <p>
      Roughly half the shops in this directory accept debit cards. The rest are cash-preferred or
      cash-only, which is a consequence of federal banking restrictions rather than a local
      quirk. Check the listing before you drive out, and expect an ATM on site if they are cash-only.
    </p>

    <h2 id="potency">Potency caps and reading a label</h2>
    <p>
      Vermont is unusual in writing potency limits into law. Flower cannot exceed
      <strong>30% THC</strong>. Solid concentrates cannot exceed <strong>60%</strong>. If you have
      shopped in Massachusetts or Maine, you will notice the top of the concentrate range is
      simply missing here.
    </p>
    <p>
      More useful than the headline THC number is the certificate of analysis, which every legal
      product carries. It lists cannabinoid content and usually a terpene breakdown. Terpenes do
      more to shape how something feels than the THC percentage does, which is why two products
      testing identically can behave nothing alike.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are new, start meaningfully lower than you expect to need, and wait. Edibles in
      particular can take two hours to arrive fully.
    </p>

    <h2 id="taxes">Taxes and paying</h2>
    <p>
      Vermont charges a <strong>14% cannabis excise tax</strong> on top of the ordinary
      <strong>6% sales tax</strong>. Together that is about 20% added at the register, and most
      dispensaries list menu prices before tax.
    </p>
    <p>
      A $40 eighth becomes roughly $48. Budget accordingly, particularly if you are paying cash.
    </p>

    <h2 id="where">Where the dispensaries are</h2>
    <p>
      There are {total} licensed retailers in this directory, spread across {towns} towns in
      {counties} counties. The distribution is lopsided, and the reason is Vermont's local opt-in rule.
    </p>
    <p>
      A dispensary cannot open in a town that has not voted to allow cannabis retail. Not zoned
      for it, not merely declined to ban it: voted yes. Towns that never took up the question are
      closed by default, and a state licence does not override that.
    </p>
    <p>
      The practical effect is clustering. Burlington has more than twenty shops. Whole stretches
      of the state have none. If your town shows nothing, check the
      <a href="/dispensaries/by-county/">county page</a> rather than assuming you have to drive far.
    </p>

    <h2 id="medical">The medical program</h2>
    <p>
      Vermont's medical cannabis program predates adult-use retail and still operates alongside it.
      Registered patients get access to some products and quantities that adult-use customers do
      not, and they are exempt from certain restrictions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Registration goes through the Cannabis Control Board and requires certification from a
      healthcare provider. If you are using cannabis to manage a diagnosed condition, it is worth
      looking into rather than defaulting to the adult-use market.
    </p>

    <h2 id="home-grow">Growing at home</h2>
    <p>
      Vermont permits two mature and four immature plants <strong>per dwelling</strong>, not per
      adult. Two adults sharing a house do not get four mature plants between them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Plants must be kept out of public view and secured from anyone under 21. Renters should check
      their lease, because landlords can and often do prohibit cultivation regardless of state law.
    </p>

    <h2 id="faq">Common questions</h2>
    {faqs.map((f) => (
      <>
        <h3>{f.q}</h3>
        <p>{f.a}</p>
      </>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="card p-6 mt-10">
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted leading-relaxed">
      <strong class="text-brand-ink">This guide is general information, not legal advice.</strong>
      Vermont's cannabis rules change, and the version that matters is the current one. The
      <a href="https://ccb.vermont.gov/" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-brand-green font-semibold hover:underline">Cannabis Control Board</a>
      publishes the regulations and the official licensee list. Last reviewed August 2026.
    </p>
  </div>
</Page>
